Warhol but not Haring'), which will now record taste. Either compose the eval's prompt through ask_system(describe_taste(...)) read from the eval server's taste, or record in the plan that the eval is deliberately left on the untasted prompt and why.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/ask/prompt.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The taste brief is put together in two places that read the affinities by different paths", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `describe_taste`'s docstring says it is the one rendering, \"so the two agents are told the same thing in the same words\", but the read that feeds it is duplicated. app.py builds Ask's brief as `describe_taste(services.taste.list_affinities())`, going through TasteService. `DiscoveryService.taste()` builds phase 1's brief as `describe_taste(self._store.list_affinities())`, going straight to the store, so DiscoveryService now owns a taste responsibility that TasteService already has. TasteService.list_affinities's docstring names itself as the place where api-contract's limit-and-report-the-total paging will land. A maintainer who adds paging there changes Ask's brief and leaves phase 1's unchanged, which is the drift the shared renderer was meant to prevent. Fix: give the brief one owner, for example `TasteService.brief()` returning `describe_taste(self.list_affinities())`. Then (a) app.py's lambda calls that method, and (b) the runner gets the same callable or the TasteService itself through its constructor in services/container.py. Retire `DiscoveryService.taste()`.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/library/services/discovery.py", "arrt/src/arrt/app.py", "arrt/src/arrt/library/services/runner.py", "arrt/src/arrt/library/services/taste.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "agent.py's module summary still calls Ask's tools read tools after ASK_SCOPE gained a write", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The first line of agent.py's docstring still says \"one thread, the surface's read tools\". ASK_SCOPE now includes `art_taste`'s `set`, which writes. api-contract.md, security-model.md and prompt.py's ASK_SCOPE comment were all updated, but this line was not. It is prose and nothing reads it. Make it relational (\"the surface's tools in ASK_SCOPE\") rather than adding a list of exceptions.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/ask/agent.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The accepted steered-write tradeoff was decided without the fact that a written judgment is replayed, at any length, into every later Ask prompt and every paid phase 1 prompt", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. security-model.md accepts that a steered page can make Ask write a `stated` judgment because 'the write spends nothing, shows in the thread as it is made, and is corrected on Taste'. That account is of the write alone. In this diff `describe_taste` renders `value` (and `rationale`) verbatim into Ask's system prompt for every later reply (`ask_system`) and into phase 1's prompt for every discovery run (`TASTE_SECTION`), and `validated_write` only strips and non-empty-checks `value` (`require_text`), so one steered write becomes standing prompt text of unbounded length in every future model call, including the paid one. The prompt shows it every time, but threads are not stored, so once the thread closes nothing shows where it came from. `TASTE_TOLD` caps rows, not size. This is what the surface rule 'When you bound a result by arithmetic, name every term that scales with the same N' warns about: the comment says 'a household's taste is tens of rows' and leaves out the characters per row. Two fixes: bound `value` (and `rationale`) length where the write is validated, or cap each line in `describe_taste`. Then extend security-model's acceptance to say a write stays in every later prompt, so the owner rules on the whole consequence and not just the write.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/library/services/taste.py", "arrt/src/arrt/ask/prompt.py", "arrt/src/arrt/library/discovery/phase_one.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "A taste write made by Ask leaves no record of where it came from once the thread is gone", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Ask's one write path is new. Detecting a write the curator never made (the steered-page case security-model accepts) relies on the thread showing 'Recording your taste: \u2026'. Threads are in memory and conversations are not stored. The service's `taste.set` log carries only kind and derivation, and Ask's reply log carries only a `tool_calls` count. So once the thread is gone, a row Ask wrote is a `stated` row with no rationale, and nothing tells it apart from one written over MCP or by the curator. An operator cannot answer 'did Ask write this, and in which thread?'. Log each write tool call Ask makes with its thread id and the tool and action (in `_Tally` or the reply's log line). Or pass an origin through to the `taste.set` log's `extra`.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/ask/agent.py", "arrt/src/arrt/library/services/taste.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "Phase 1's log does not say what it was told", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The `engine.completed` log line records tokens and works returned, but not how many taste lines or rejected works the request carried, or whether `REJECTED_TOLD` or `TASTE_TOLD` cut either list. Investigating 'why did phase 1 propose a work already rejected' or 'why did input tokens grow' then means rebuilding the request by hand. Adding two counts to that `extra` would answer both. This is optional, and no fix is required for the merge.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/library/discovery/phase_one.py"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions and alignment with the plan", "recommendation": "Scope: none, for the owner. The plan records four mid-build decisions, each with what settled it: the Taste help gains 'tell Ask', because the help claims to list every way; the brief orders loves, declines, likes, cool, so a cut keeps the strongest; the request is built inside the runner's guard, because it now reads the catalogue, and a test pins this (a catalogue that cannot be read fails the run); and `REJECTED_TOLD` = 100, one per dedup key. Each matches the code. Alignment-pass misses: Chunk 01 lists two deliverables the diff has no hunk for. (1) 'the eval composing the prompt the same way': `arrt/tests/eval/ask.py` and `test_ask_requests.py` still pass `ASK_SYSTEM`, not `ask_system(...)`, so the eval measures only the no-taste prompt, while its docstring says 'what is measured is what ships'. (2) 'card cases for the two actions': `arrt/ask/cards.py` is unchanged. Either build them or record them as descoped in the plan. The plan's 'Inferred' goals are labelled as such and say the boundary review shows them, so the owner should see them here: set-only (no delete), stated-only, the request outranks the taste, and the rejected list is capped.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-taste-in-ask.md"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings Cross-Check", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Read list from `learnings-files --for-diff`: `.claude/rules/learnings/core.md` and `.claude/rules/learnings/surface.md`. One rule fired: 'When you bound a result by arithmetic, name every term that scales with the same N'. It is folded into the steered-write finding, since `TASTE_TOLD` bounds rows and not their length. The change follows several rules: 'When a result gains a collection, write down what bounds it' (both caps carry their reasoning); 'When a clause or notice is conditional, assert its absence' (the phase 1 tests assert each section is absent when empty, and Ask's test asserts the bare `ASK_SYSTEM` prompt); and 'When a comment names a failure as unacceptable' (the runner's comment about a held run is tested through a taste read that raises). No learnings rule was added or changed this cycle. No rule was found that is written down but has no check enforcing it.", "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog Reconciliation", "recommendation": "Scope: none. `backlog cache-query --repo brookstalley/arrt affecting` returned 0 items for the changed source files (cache confirmed 2026-11-08T18:13Z). No open item mentions taste, affinity, rejection or phase 1. The only Ask-related one, #402 (a flaky browser test), is not resolved by this change. No item appears resolved, and there are no hygiene signals (no backlog items were created in the interval).", "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "Records Pass: all seven oracle files covered, none clears a bar", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Covered: agents-and-memory.md, api-contract.md, build-plan-taste-in-ask.md, data-model.md, information-architecture.md, security-model.md, changes/taste-in-ask.md. Checked against the code: the service refuses `observed` and `inferred` (`set_affinity` and `validated_write`); `delete` is absent from `ASK_SCOPE`; the thread line 'Recording your taste: \u2026' exists in `_SAID`; Ask's prompt is rebuilt every reply; phase 1 carries the taste and the newest `REJECTED_TOLD` rejections; and the IA's Taste help row matches taste.js. Nothing ships false, misleads into action, or must stop the merge. Two gaps are carried by the findings above and add no record finding of their own: security-model's incomplete acceptance and the plan's two deliverables with no hunk. record_lint reported 0 findings.", "fid": "R-10"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 4, "note": 6}, "observations": [], "duration_seconds": 420, "scope": "taste-in-ask", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_ext